      "writtenOn": "2026-10-26T23:37:06Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Thanks for looking into a smaller fix. Resetting the counter when `*index \u003d\u003d 0` handles the repeated iteration, but the counter still grows on every call with `*index \u003e 0`, and those calls are not limited to one per output frame: the caller can retrieve the same output frame again with a copy of the iterator. With AV1D_SET_OUTPUT_ALL_LAYERS and two spatial layers, the fourth retrieval of output frame 1 through a copy of the iterator writes `grain_image_frame_buffers[4]`, that is, over `num_grain_image_frame_buffers`, `need_resync` and `buffer_pool`.\n\nPatch set 3 adds that case to the test. I ran it with your diff applied (ASan + UBSan build): each copy gets a new grain image, and the fifth retrieval returns NULL because `need_resync` was overwritten. Patch set 3 passes it.\n\nIndexing the slot by the output frame index bounds it by construction (`*index \u003c pbi-\u003enum_output_frames \u003c\u003d MAX_NUM_SPATIAL_LAYERS`), however the frames are retrieved. The rest of the size is that a second retrieval of the same output frame returns the grain image already made, so the planes of an image the caller already has stay valid until the next `aom_codec_decode()` call, as they do for frames without film grain, instead of being released when the iteration restarts (with external frame buffers, a released buffer goes back to the application). That needs one `aom_image_t` per output frame (`images_with_grain[]`). The two small helpers replace the two copies of the release loop.",
